 a master  in Social Work because you prevent a lot  of crime what it straight out just you  prevent having to deal with crime then  we'll get to how you how do you deal  with crime you prevent a lot of crime if  you have police that are properly  trained  as social workers in addition there's a  lot of stuff that's not actually  criminal or it's marginally criminalized  as misdemeanors that can or cannot be  enforced based on the discretion of  officers we're if you have officers  better trained in de-escalation you have  officers better trained with some of  these soft skills as we might call them  you reduce the number of interactions  that become problematic  number two we need more extensive  psychological and personality testing of  police and we want to just make sure  we're bringing in the right people again  most cops are good some like Trump  admitted on Twitter they're looking for  action there's a sort of bloodlust in  some fraction of police officers those  officers understand this and I don't I  don't want to get off topic here the bad  apples the bad eggs don't just typically  do one bad thing they do a whole bunch  so a huge portion of the over overuse of  force complaints and other bad acts are  concentrated among a small number of  repeat police offenders if we did a  better job of evaluating and psych  testing and personality testing police  we could root out a lot of the problem  by never hiring that small percentage  that wants to get out there and slap  people around and is looking to get the  riot gear and do what we've seen being  done on videos so route them out with it  more psychological and personality  testing there should probably be some  established process for civilian review  of shoot police shootings and uses of  force it can't just be left to police  departments to investigate because we  all know that there are many police  chiefs that are doing this properly we  know that there are internal internal  affairs boards that are operating more  or less okay we should probably have  more civilian involvement in review of  police shootings and uses of force we've  got to demilitarize the militarization  of police forces has been a huge problem  I've talked about that on its own so I  won't delve too much into that and there  should probably be a national database  of bad police actors because it would  prevent two things number one there's a  national database of bad actors people  police officers with a  number of complaints against them it  would prevent officers from hiding prior  complaints by simply not reporting them  when they're trying to get hired at a  new Police Department because there  would be a national database so  reporting to the database should be  mandatory but also if you're hiring a  police officer if you're not bringing  them through police academy and you're  giving them their first police job it  should be a requirement that police  departments have to check with that  central database so that the police  department can't get away with saying we  didn't know we just didn't know because  it would be a requirement to report  complaints to the database and to look  at the database if you are hiring  someone now then you've got prosecution  in terms of prosecution decisions on  prosecution of police officers and the  prosecution itself meaning the trials it  should be happening  ideally outside the county where the  incidents took place it can be very  difficult to get what we would all call  a fair trial fair to the victims fair to  the officers fair to everybody fair to  the community when a lot of these cases  are being tried inside the jurisdiction  now by the jurist it might be outside of  the town but still in the county and on  a lot of America that's not far enough  that's another thing that should should  be done you've got to try and prosecute  these cases outside  realistically of the county where they  took place some ideas there are many  more I'd love to hear fr